Z||000000||FieroRacer||11-08-2001||09:18 PM||||As you all may know, I have lost a bearing or two from my GT's crankshaft.  After asking around, there are no shops that want to work on a fiero (Saying it takes removing the engine to even do an oil change).  I can't pull the engine due to living at an apartment.  I need to know how to remove the crankshaft from the engine with it still in the car.  What's the best procedure?||||reg||11
Z||000001||CanadianGT||11-08-2001||09:27 PM||||Sorry to say this man, but you have to remove the engine.<BR>PeAcE<BR>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000002||JohnF||11-08-2001||10:05 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by FieroRacer:<BR><B>...I need to know how to remove the crankshaft from the engine with it still in the car.  What's the best procedure?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No doubt about it!  Dynamite works.  Seriously, ANY GM new car dealer will be happy to change your oil.  <P>Sorry to say, the only real, permanent fix to a spun rod bearing is a new/rebuilt/reliably used engine or rebuild the one you have.<P>You are $500 (you do it)to $2000 (hire it done) away from having a good running Fiero.<P>Good luck.<P>||||reg||9
Z||000003||FieroRacer||11-08-2001||10:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JohnF:<BR><B> Sorry to say, the only real, permanent fix to a spun rod bearing is a new/rebuilt/reliably used engine or rebuild the one you have.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>What I will do, Depending on the condition of the crank, will either buy a new crankshaft (Already got new bearings), regrind the current one, or just budget to get a 3.4 Pushrod or another drop in engine to replace it.  Due to the urgency of need, I hope to be able to at worse swap the crank.  Looks possible if I can get the timing chain cover off (Had a friend rebuild the whole bottom end of a 67 mustang with it still in the car).||||reg||1
Z||000004||DRH||11-08-2001||10:32 PM||||It seems your biggest problem would be removing the tranny. I think the recommended way (for manuals anyway) involves dropping the cradle. If you do that, you might as well drop the engine with it. Is it auto or manual and what year? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroRacer||11-08-2001||11:27 PM||||Automatic 85.  I've been reading the chiltons and haynes, it is possible if I can get in there.  I have one last shop to try tomorrow.  If that doesn't work, I'll have no choice to do it the long hard way.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||11-09-2001||05:22 AM||||It can be done, but it won't be easy!<BR>At least an automatic is easier than a manual car.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Leadfoot||11-09-2001||11:07 AM||||Fieroracer, if you do it yourself give it plenty of time.  My swap took a month because I was either waiting for parts or fixing broken items.  Usually the cradle bolts rust solid and creates a wrench throwing/cussing fest.  If yours are rusted I would suggest a dremmel tool to cut the bolts.  If you don't have them rent a hoist and engine stand.  Once you have the engine out take plenty of pictures of it an label where every bolt goes.  This will save you a bunch of time when you go to assemble the new engine.  Also, replace the exhaust studs with a set from Saturn.  You can find the part number(s) by doing a search.  Best of luck to you!||||reg||1
